  "conclusion": "The retained judgments support relationship-specific ownership analysis, not a general rule that exchange customers beneficially own deposited cryptocurrency. The 2023 judgment recognises cryptocurrency as property capable of being held on trust. An actual express trust still requires certainty of subject matter, beneficiaries and intention. Identified fungible pools and ledger-recorded proportions can supply the first two; intention is assessed objectively from the agreement and relationship. Trust language, agency, pooling, ledger references to ownership and fiduciary disclaimers are relevant, but none alone determines title.\n\nThe revised contractual regime governed customers who accepted it through relevant Platform access or use, except qualifying Non-consenting Customers. Removal of trust language, disclaimers of fiduciary capacity and Gatecoin's entitlement to accretions supported holding the currencies in its own right. Earlier trust arrangements remained relevant for the exception. The 2025 judgment develops that exception into beneficial co-ownership of each relevant cryptocurrency or fiat pool according to recorded balances. Mixing does not automatically defeat a trust, and a customer need not identify the very fungible units originally deposited. Conversely, technical control or a recorded ownership entry does not independently establish beneficial ownership.\n\nCustomer consent therefore cannot be inferred from every post-change transaction. The later judgment distinguishes automated credits and Gatecoin adjustments from customer access or use, and allows proof of earlier limit-order instructions. Its broader narrative, detailed transaction discussions and annexure require careful comparison rather than a universal rule that either every entry establishes assent or every automated execution preserves trust rights.\n\nDistinct party relationships produced distinct results. BlueFire traded on Gatecoin's behalf with Gatecoin's funds and instructions; the court held its recorded balances to be Gatecoin's assets. Its failure to claim was not the sole basis. Counsel's purpose-trust analysis supported that conclusion without establishing a separate general holding. Dan's fiduciary obligations to Samara did not establish a trust binding Gatecoin: the later judgment rejected Samara's trust claim against Gatecoin, classified the specified account outside the non-consenting category and treated the remaining claim as unsecured after execution recoveries. ETD represented breach-of-trust claims for hacked ETH, described by the court as proprietary; recovery of trust property was nevertheless distinguished from equitable compensation where the missing assets could not be restored.\n\nThe earlier application presented distribution proposals rather than finally determining them. The later judgment supplies pooled-allocation directions, while leaving costs apportionment for further consideration. Those directions establish permitted allocation, not actual delivery, full recovery or a final net entitlement.\n\nTransfer to a Product Provider under 26EC22 requires identifying the precise judicial proposition and matching its legally material premises, not merely similar token technology or ledger terminology. Separately, the supplied circular retains ultimate provider responsibility for tokenisation management, operational soundness and ownership record keeping despite outsourcing. It requires proper ownership records, service-provider compatibility and technology-risk controls, with confirmation and request-dependent evidential obligations. Allocation of responsibility, supervision undertaken, operational outcomes, legal title and customer assent remain distinct. A completed check, reconciliation or solver output proves neither legal ownership nor regulatory compliance by itself. Both prior proposals incorrectly treated some now-delivered substantive authority as absent; their remaining warnings about incomplete later treatment and textual tensions remain material.",
